    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mongol khanate founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> after he destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258.</li><li>Under Ghazan it became a Persianised Islamic state whose reforms, trade and court scholarship linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</li><li>When Abu Said died without an heir in 1335 it broke into rival successor states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chupanids">Chupanids</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a>'s Jami al-Tawarikh, written at the Ilkhanid court with Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants, is often called the first universal history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

