    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hulagu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan.jpg" alt="Hulagu Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulagu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid-al-Din Hamadani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ilkhanate emerged from the westward expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> under the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In 1253 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongke">Great Khan Möngke</a>, Hulagu's brother, ordered him to subdue the remaining Muslim powers of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, assembling one of the largest Mongol armies ever fielded. Hulagu destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismaili state</a> in 1256, securing the surrender of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imam">imam</a> and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-alamut">Siege of Alamut</a>, the fortress itself, then advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. In February 1258 Mongol forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> and executed the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-mustasim">al-Mustaʿsim</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> of Baghdad that had ruled since 750. The conquest established Mongol authority across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a>. Hulagu made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maragha">Maragha</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> his capital, later supplanted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tabriz">Tabriz</a>, and adopted the title “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhan">Ilkhan</a>”, usually understood as a subordinate khan acknowledging the supremacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-khan">Great Khan</a>. The new state rested on Mongol military dominance but relied heavily on the administrative traditions of the conquered Persian lands, a tension that would shape its development.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tabriz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabriz.jpg" alt="Tabriz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tabriz">Tabriz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabriz_Municipality_Sa%27at_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">user:Elmju, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ilkhanate governed a vast and diverse population, composed largely of Persian agricultural communities, urban artisans and merchants integrated into Eurasian trade networks. Mongol elites formed a small ruling minority, often living as nomadic aristocrats while Persian bureaucrats administered taxation. Agriculture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> suffered severe disruption from the Mongol campaigns and the spread of pastoral land use, which damaged irrigation systems and depopulated rural districts. Recovery began around the turn of the fourteenth century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazan-khan">Ghazan Khan</a> (r. 1295–1304), whose fiscal reforms stabilised revenue collection and protected peasants from arbitrary exactions by nomadic elites. Trade flourished along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a>, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and the Ilkhanate also benefited from diplomatic and commercial contacts with European powers seeking allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluks</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultaniyya">Sultaniyya</a> became major commercial hubs where Mongol, Persian, Armenian, Genoese and Venetian merchants met within a revitalised transcontinental economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut.jpg" alt="Battle of Ain Jalut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campaign_of_the_Battle_of_Ain_Jalut_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MapMaster, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ilkhanid military inherited the highly mobile cavalry structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, built around disciplined units of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> capable of rapid manoeuvre and co-ordinated battlefield tactics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>'s army demonstrated overwhelming effectiveness in the initial conquests, above all at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258. Mongol expansion met a decisive limit, however, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qutuz">Sultan Qutuz</a> and the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baybars">Baybars</a> defeated a Mongol force commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galilee">Galilee</a>. The defeat fixed a western frontier along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euphrates">Euphrates</a> that the Ilkhanate never overcame. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhan">Ilkhans</a> launched repeated campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and Ghazan's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-wadi-al-khaznadar">Battle of Wadi al-Khaznadar</a> in 1299, which briefly delivered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a>, was their only major field victory in the conflict; it was reversed at Marj al-Saffar in 1303. Logistical difficulties and the resilience of Mamluk military institutions prevented lasting gains, and military power, though central to Ilkhanid legitimacy, gradually shifted from conquest to defence and internal control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jami-al-tawarikh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jami-al-tawarikh.jpg" alt="Jami' al-Tawarikh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jami-al-tawarikh">Jami' al-Tawarikh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_soldiers_by_Rashid_al-Din_1305.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Governance in the Ilkhanate reflected a synthesis of Mongol imperial tradition and Persian bureaucratic administration. Early rulers maintained Mongol legal customs derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yasa">Yasa</a> while relying on experienced Persian officials to manage taxation, land surveys and record keeping. The most significant transformation came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazan-khan">Mahmud Ghazan</a>, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> in 1295 and implemented extensive reforms with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a>. These reforms standardised coinage, weights and measures, regulated taxation, curbed abuses by military elites and reorganised provincial administration. Ghazan also built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and postal relay stations to facilitate trade and communication. The bureaucracy became increasingly Persianised, reflecting the demographic reality of the realm. Rashid al-Din's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jami-al-tawarikh">Jamiʿ al-Tawarikh</a></em>, produced at the Ilkhanid court, is often described as the first universal history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>, drawing on Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants. Governance thus evolved from a conquest regime into a structured state apparatus integrating Mongol authority with Persian administrative expertise.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#dome-of-soltaniyeh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-soltaniyeh.jpg" alt="Dome of Soltaniyeh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#dome-of-soltaniyeh">Dome of Soltaniyeh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dome_of_Soltaniyeh_jb1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jb-mhr, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ilkhanate played a significant role in connecting the Islamic world with the wider Mongol imperial system spanning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>. Embassies to European monarchs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a>, including the mission of the monk Rabban Sauma in 1287–1288, sought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-mongol-alliance">Franco-Mongol alliance</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluks</a>, though these efforts never produced effective military co-operation. Trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> facilitated the movement of goods, technologies and artistic influences between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Ilkhanid culture blended Mongol, Persian and Chinese traditions, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript-illumination">manuscript illumination</a> and architecture, a synthesis exemplified by the great domed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dome-of-soltaniyeh">mausoleum at Sultaniyya</a> built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oljeitu">Öljeitü</a>, completed around 1313. The Ilkhanate also influenced the religious landscape of Persia: Öljeitü's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#twelver-shiism">Twelver Shiʿism</a> briefly promoted the sect within state structures, an early precedent for the Safavid establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiism">Shiʿism</a> as Persia's official religion in 1501. Through these cultural and economic exchanges, the Ilkhanate served as a conduit linking several major civilisations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#oljeitu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oljeitu.jpg" alt="Öljeitü" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#oljeitu">Öljeitü</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khan_Oljaitu_accepts_Yuan_ambassadors,_as_featured_in_Majma%27_al-Tavarikh_(cropped)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ilkhanate's decline resulted primarily from dynastic instability and political fragmentation. After Ghazan's death in 1304 the empire retained much of its administrative structure, but successive rulers struggled to maintain centralised authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oljeitu">Öljeitü</a>'s conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiism">Shiʿism</a> created religious tensions within a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> population and Mongol elite. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-said-bahadur-khan">Abu Saʿid</a> Bahadur Khan came to the throne as a minor in 1317, leaving effective power with powerful military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir">emirs</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chupan">Amir Chupan</a>, whom he eventually overthrew in 1327. Court factions competed for influence throughout the reign, weakening institutional stability, and the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> in 1318 removed the state's most capable administrator. When Abu Saʿid died in 1335 without an heir, the Ilkhanate fragmented into rival successor states controlled by regional dynasties, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chupanids">Chupanids</a>, who ruled through puppet khans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-death">Black Death</a> further weakened the region, and by the mid-fourteenth century effective Mongol political authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> had disappeared.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ilkhanate stands as a striking example of how a Mongol conquest state evolved into a Persianised Islamic empire whose enduring influence lay less in territorial expansion than in the cultural and administrative synthesis it created across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</em></p>

